{"name":"Smoking Mirror","hex":"#a29587","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"smoking","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of smoke"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Giving off smoke.","Sexually attractive, usually referring to a woman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoking"},{"term":"mirror","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A smooth surface, usually made of glass with reflective material painted on the underside, that reflects light so as to give an image of what is in front of it.","An object, person, or event that reflects or gives a picture of another."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reflect, as in a mirror.","To act as a reflection of, either by being identical to, or by being identical but reversed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mirror"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tezcatlipoca","blocks":[{"text":"Tezcatlipoca was a central deity in Aztec religion. He is associated with a variety of concepts, including the night sky, hurricanes, obsidian, and conflict. He was considered one of the four sons of Ometecuhtli and Omecihuatl, the primordial dual deity. His main festival was Toxcatl, which, like most religious festivals of Aztec culture, involved human sacrifice."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Tezcatlipoca.png/330px-Tezcatlipoca.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tezcatlipoca","wikidata":"Q756797"},{"title":"Smoking","blocks":[{"text":"Smoking is the combustion of a substance, usually plant material, and the inhalation of resulting fumes. Today, smoking is mostly practiced by rolling the dried leaves of the tobacco plant into a cigarette. Other forms of tobacco smoking include the use of smoking tobacco with a pipe or cigar, or using a bong. Cigarette smokers almost always inhale the smoke; most pipe and cigar smokers do not inhale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Smoke_%2834942422652%29.jpg/330px-Smoke_%2834942422652%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoking","wikidata":"Q662860"},{"title":"Mirror","blocks":[{"text":"A mirror, also known as a looking glass, is an object that reflects an image. Light that bounces off a mirror forms an image of whatever is in front of it, which is then focused through the lens of the eye or a camera. Mirrors reverse the direction of light at an angle equal to its incidence. This allows the viewer to see themselves or objects behind them, or even objects that are at an angle from them but out of their field of view, such as around a corner. Natural mirrors have existed since prehistoric times, such as the surface of water, but people have been manufacturing mirrors out of a variety of materials for thousands of years, like stone, metals, and glass. In modern mirrors, metals like silver or aluminium are often used due to their high reflectivity, applied as a thin coating on glass because of its naturally smooth and hard surface."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror","wikidata":"Q35197"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Standing_jaguar.jpg/500px-Standing_jaguar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Standing_jaguar.jpg/500px-Standing_jaguar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The jaguar was an animal sacred to Tezcatlipoca.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStanding_jaguar.jpg%23%2Fmedia"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/John_Dee%27s_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg/500px-John_Dee%27s_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/John_Dee%27s_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg/1280px-John_Dee%27s_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/John_Dee%27s_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg/500px-John_Dee%27s_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aztec obsidian mirror","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Dee's_mirror_British_Museum_26_07_2013.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Black_Tezcatlipoca.jpg/500px-Black_Tezcatlipoca.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Black_Tezcatlipoca.jpg/1280px-Black_Tezcatlipoca.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Black_Tezcatlipoca.jpg/500px-Black_Tezcatlipoca.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tezcatlipoca with all 20 day signs, symbolizing the divine calendar (Codex Borgia)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Tezcatlipoca.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Codex_Borgia_page_12.jpg/500px-Codex_Borgia_page_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Codex_Borgia_page_12.jpg/500px-Codex_Borgia_page_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACodex_Borgia_page_12.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu